  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "adr-llm-provider-strategy-1",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "ADR: Hybrid LLM routing by task",
      "width": 180,
      "height": 52,
      "collapsed": false,
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "status": "proposed",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Every model call has to go somewhere: a hosted frontier API, a self-hosted open-weight model, or a mix. The right choice depends on task quality requirements, per-token cost at expected volume, latency, and whether the input data is allowed to leave the perimeter at all.",
      "decision": "Default to a hosted frontier API for quality-critical, low-volume tasks (drafting, complex reasoning). Route high-volume, latency-sensitive or data-sensitive tasks (classification, extraction, redaction) to a self-hosted open-weight model behind the same internal completion interface, so callers don't know or care which one answered.",
      "consequences": "• Quality-critical tasks keep pace with frontier model improvements without any migration; only the routing table changes as models are added.\n• High-volume tasks avoid per-token cost that would otherwise dominate the bill, at the cost of running and updating GPU infrastructure for the self-hosted model.\n• Data that cannot leave the perimeter has a compliant path (self-hosted) without blocking the product entirely on it.\n• The routing layer and its fallback behaviour — what happens when the preferred provider is down or rate-limited — becomes critical infrastructure in its own right, not an afterthought.",
      "alternatives": "1. Always hosted API — simplest to build and keeps pace with frontier quality, but every request leaves the perimeter and cost scales linearly with volume regardless of task complexity.\n2. Always self-hosted — full control over data residency and cost at scale, but requires GPU operations expertise and typically lags frontier quality on complex reasoning tasks.\n3. Single hosted provider, no fallback — simplest integration, but a provider outage or pricing change has no mitigation.\n4. Hybrid routing by task (chosen) — matches each task to the provider it actually needs, at the cost of maintaining two integration paths and a routing policy."
    }
  ]
}
